WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.460
A private RAG system has many moving parts: source connectors, ingestion jobs, parsers,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.840
chunkers, embedding generation, vector stores, metadata stores, retrieval services,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.000
model endpoints, user interfaces, logs, evaluation jobs, and administration workflows.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.045
Each component needs ownership and failure behavior.

00:00:17.045 --> 00:00:19.000
Operations starts with refresh and lifecycle planning.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.995
Sources change, documents expire, permissions move, and old guidance is replaced.

00:00:22.995 --> 00:00:27.570
The system should define refresh schedules, incremental indexing, deletion workflows,

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.000
stale-content handling, and review for sources that fail to update.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.995
A RAG system that cannot remove or update knowledge reliably will lose trust.

00:00:34.995 --> 00:00:39.000
Reliability planning should cover source outages, parser failures, embedding failures,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.720
index corruption, model unavailability, slow retrieval, storage limits,

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:43.975
and failed background jobs.

00:00:43.975 --> 00:00:48.265
Users may not see these components directly, but their answers depend on them.

00:00:48.265 --> 00:00:52.190
Monitoring and alerts should make important failures visible before users quietly receive

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:53.000
poor results.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Versioning helps teams understand change.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.565
Models, prompts, indexes, chunking rules, retrieval settings,

00:00:58.565 --> 00:01:00.972
and evaluation sets may all affect output.

00:01:00.972 --> 00:01:03.850
Recording versions makes it easier to compare behavior, explain changes,

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:04.995
and roll back when needed.

00:01:04.995 --> 00:01:07.000
Reliability is also a governance concern.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.915
Users may rely on retrieved answers for real work, so backup, restore, rollback,

00:01:11.915 --> 00:01:16.170
cost controls, incident response, and communication plans belong in the architecture.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.995
Private RAG becomes dependable when operations are planned, owned, and tested.

00:01:19.995 --> 00:01:24.170
Operational runbooks should name owners, normal recovery steps, escalation paths,

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:25.995
and communication expectations.

00:01:25.995 --> 00:01:28.000
When retrieval quality drops or a connector fails,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.000
the team should know whether to pause the workflow, serve degraded results,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.990
or direct users to source systems.
